-- Q1: easy / L2 — Assembled workforce not separately recognized
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Harmon Corp. acquires 100% of Vista Inc. in a business combination. Vista has a highly trained engineering team with specialized skills. How should Harmon account for the value of Vista's assembled workforce?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Include its value in goodwill", "Recognize it as a separate identifiable intangible asset", "Expense it immediately as compensation cost", "Record it as an other asset outside of goodwill"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): ASC 805-20-55-6. An assembled workforce is not recognized as a separate identifiable intangible asset because it does not meet the contractual-legal or separability criteria. Its value is subsumed within goodwill. Wrong (B): Assembled workforce fails both the contractual-legal and separability criteria required for separate recognition under ASC 805. Wrong (C): Assembled workforce value is not expensed; it is captured in the residual goodwill amount. Wrong (D): There is no "other asset" classification for workforce value; it is included in goodwill by default.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Preexisting relationship settlement
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Prior to acquiring Zenith Corp., Dalton Inc. had an ongoing lawsuit against Zenith seeking $3,000,000 in damages. At the acquisition date, Dalton acquires 100% of Zenith and the lawsuit is effectively settled. The fair value of the settlement is $2,200,000, which is favorable to Dalton. How should Dalton account for this settlement in the business combination?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Include the $2,200,000 settlement as part of the consideration transferred", "Recognize a $2,200,000 gain separately from the business combination", "Reduce goodwill by $2,200,000", "Recognize the $2,200,000 as an identifiable intangible asset"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): ASC 805-10-25-20. When a business combination effectively settles a preexisting relationship between the acquirer and acquiree, the settlement is accounted for separately from the combination. A favorable settlement results in a gain recognized in earnings. Wrong (A): The settlement is not part of the exchange for the business and must be separated from the consideration transferred. Wrong (C): Goodwill is not adjusted for preexisting relationship settlements; they are separate transactions. Wrong (D): A lawsuit settlement is not an identifiable intangible asset of the acquiree.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Share-based replacement awards allocation
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Beacon Corp. acquires Lark Inc. and replaces Lark's unvested stock options with Beacon replacement awards. The acquisition-date fair value of the replacement awards is $900,000. The portion attributable to pre-combination service is $600,000. How should Beacon account for the replacement awards?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Expense the entire $900,000 as post-combination compensation", "Include $600,000 in consideration transferred and expense $300,000 as post-combination compensation", "Include the entire $900,000 in the consideration transferred", "Recognize $600,000 as a liability and $300,000 as equity"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): ASC 805-30-30-9 through 30-11. When the acquirer is obligated to replace the acquiree's awards, the portion attributable to pre-combination service ($600,000) is included in the consideration transferred. The remaining $300,000 is recognized as post-combination compensation expense over the remaining service period. Wrong (A): Only the post-combination portion ($300,000) is expensed; the pre-combination portion is part of the purchase price. Wrong (C): Including the entire amount in consideration ignores the post-combination service component that must be expensed separately. Wrong (D): Replacement awards are not split between liability and equity in this manner.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Post-combination restructuring costs
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Under ASC 805, how should an acquirer account for restructuring costs it plans to incur after a business combination to reorganize the acquiree's operations?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Capitalize them as part of goodwill", "Include them in the consideration transferred", "Accrue them as an assumed liability at the acquisition date", "Expense them in the periods incurred"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): ASC 805-10-25-23. Restructuring costs that the acquirer expects to incur but is not obligated to incur at the acquisition date are not recognized as part of the business combination. They are expensed in the post-combination periods in which they are incurred. Wrong (A): Restructuring plans of the acquirer are not capitalized to goodwill. Wrong (B): These costs are not part of the exchange price for the business. Wrong (C): The acquirer cannot accrue a restructuring liability at the acquisition date for its own plans; only the acquiree's pre-existing obligations qualify.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Deferred tax from fair value step-up
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Pryor Corp. acquires 100% of Summit Inc. for $10,000,000. Summit's identifiable net assets have a book value of $6,000,000 and a fair value of $8,500,000. The fair value step-up of $2,500,000 relates to non-deductible intangible assets (tax basis remains $6,000,000). The tax rate is 25%. What amount of goodwill should Pryor recognize?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$2,125,000", "$1,500,000", "$2,750,000", "$1,000,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): ASC 805-740-25-2. The fair value step-up creates a deferred tax liability of $2,500,000 × 25% = $625,000. Net identifiable assets = $8,500,000 − $625,000 = $7,875,000. Goodwill = $10,000,000 − $7,875,000 = $2,125,000. The DTL reduces net assets, increasing goodwill. Wrong (B): This ignores the deferred tax liability entirely ($10,000,000 − $8,500,000 = $1,500,000). Wrong (C): This incorrectly adds the DTL to the purchase price instead of subtracting it from net assets. Wrong (D): This uses an incorrect tax calculation.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: hard / L3 — Bargain purchase with NCI present
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Atlas Corp. acquires 80% of Brix Inc. for $7,200,000. The fair value of the noncontrolling interest is $1,800,000. The fair value of Brix's identifiable net assets is $10,500,000. After reassessing all assets and liabilities, the excess remains. What should Atlas report?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Goodwill of $1,500,000 on the consolidated balance sheet", "A gain on bargain purchase of $1,500,000 in earnings", "Negative goodwill allocated to reduce noncurrent assets", "A gain on bargain purchase of $1,200,000 in earnings"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): ASC 805-30-25-2. Consideration ($7,200,000) + NCI ($1,800,000) = $9,000,000, which is less than net identifiable assets of $10,500,000. After re-examination, the excess of $1,500,000 is recognized as a gain on bargain purchase in earnings. Wrong (A): The result is negative, not positive, so no goodwill exists. Wrong (C): Under ASC 805, negative goodwill is not allocated to reduce assets; it is recognized as a gain immediately. Wrong (D): This incorrectly uses only the acquirer's 80% share ($10,500,000 × 80% − $7,200,000) instead of including NCI.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — Contractual-legal criterion for intangible recognition
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Under ASC 805, which criterion allows an intangible asset to be recognized separately from goodwill even if it cannot be sold independently?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The fair value criterion", "The historical cost criterion", "The contractual-legal criterion", "The reliability criterion"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): ASC 805-20-55-2. An intangible asset meets the identifiability criterion if it arises from contractual or other legal rights, regardless of whether it can be transferred or separated from the entity. This is one of two paths to separate recognition. Wrong (A): Fair value is a measurement attribute, not a recognition criterion for identifying intangible assets. Wrong (B): Historical cost is not a recognition criterion under ASC 805's identifiability framework. Wrong (D): Reliability is a general measurement concept, not one of the two ASC 805 identifiability criteria.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Goodwill subsequent accounting (public entities)
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Under U.S. GAAP for public business entities, how is goodwill accounted for in periods subsequent to a business combination?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Amortized over 10 years on a straight-line basis", "Amortized over its estimated useful life, not to exceed 40 years", "Written off immediately in the period following the acquisition", "Not amortized but tested for impairment at least annually"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): ASC 350-20-35-1. For public business entities, goodwill is not amortized. Instead, it is tested for impairment at the reporting unit level at least annually and whenever events indicate impairment may exist. Wrong (A): The 10-year straight-line method is not required under current U.S. GAAP for goodwill. Wrong (B): The 40-year amortization limit applied under the superseded APB Opinion No. 17, not current standards. Wrong (C): Goodwill is retained on the balance sheet indefinitely unless impaired; it is not written off immediately.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — Post-combination compensation vs contingent consideration
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Kline Corp. acquires 100% of Foster Inc. The purchase agreement requires Foster's former CEO to remain employed for three years and provides a $2,000,000 payment contingent on continued employment. The CEO held 60% of Foster's shares prior to the acquisition. Under ASC 805, how should Kline most likely classify this arrangement?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Post-combination compensation expense over the service period", "Contingent consideration included in the acquisition-date purchase price", "A liability assumed in the business combination measured at fair value", "An equity-classified component of the consideration transferred"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): ASC 805-10-55-24 through 55-25. When a payment is contingent on continued employment, it is a strong indicator that the arrangement is compensation for post-combination services, not contingent consideration. This is expensed over the required service period. Wrong (B): Employment-contingent payments are generally not contingent consideration; the employment requirement indicates compensation. Wrong (C): This is not an assumed liability of the acquiree; it arises from the acquirer's arrangement with the former owner. Wrong (D): Compensation arrangements are not classified as equity consideration in the purchase price allocation.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Indemnification asset recognition
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Reed Corp. acquires 100% of Grant Inc. The seller agrees to indemnify Reed for any losses from a specific pending tax dispute, up to $1,500,000. At the acquisition date, Reed recognizes the tax liability at its fair value of $1,100,000. What amount should Reed recognize for the indemnification asset?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No indemnification asset should be recognized", "An indemnification asset of $1,100,000", "An indemnification asset of $1,500,000", "An indemnification asset of $400,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): ASC 805-20-25-27. The acquirer recognizes an indemnification asset at the same time and on the same basis as the indemnified item. Since the tax liability is recognized at $1,100,000, the indemnification asset is also recognized at $1,100,000, measured consistently with the indemnified liability. Wrong (A): When the seller provides contractual indemnification, the asset must be recognized. Wrong (C): The indemnification asset is measured at the same amount as the recognized liability, not at the maximum cap of $1,500,000. Wrong (D): The $400,000 difference between the cap and liability is not the measurement basis for the asset.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Business vs. asset acquisition distinction
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Wren Corp. purchases a group of assets from another entity. The acquired set includes real estate, a lease portfolio, and an assembled workforce, but no processes or outputs that could produce revenue. Under ASC 805, how should Wren most likely account for this transaction?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["As a business combination using the acquisition method", "As a business combination with goodwill recognition", "As an asset acquisition with cost allocated to individual assets", "As a business combination with a bargain purchase gain"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): ASC 805-10-55-5A. If substantially all of the fair value is concentrated in a single asset or group of similar assets, or if the set lacks an input and a substantive process that together produce outputs, the transaction is an asset acquisition, not a business combination. Cost is allocated to individual assets based on relative fair values. Wrong (A): Without substantive processes, the set does not meet the definition of a business. Wrong (B): Goodwill is only recognized in business combinations, not asset acquisitions. Wrong (D): Bargain purchase gains apply only to business combinations under ASC 805.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Acquiree deferred revenue fair value
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Marion Corp. acquires Yates Inc., which has deferred revenue of $4,000,000 (book value) related to annual service contracts. The estimated cost to fulfill the remaining performance obligation is $2,800,000, and a normal profit margin for similar services is 10%. What is the acquisition-date fair value of the assumed deferred revenue liability?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$4,000,000", "$2,800,000", "$2,520,000", "$3,080,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): ASC 805-20-30-1. The deferred revenue liability is measured at fair value, which approximates the cost to fulfill ($2,800,000) plus a normal profit margin ($2,800,000 × 10% = $280,000) = $3,080,000. The acquirer remeasures the obligation at what a market participant would require to assume it. Wrong (A): The book value of deferred revenue is not the fair value; it must be remeasured. Wrong (B): Cost to fulfill alone omits the profit margin a market participant would demand. Wrong (C): This incorrectly reduces the cost by the margin instead of adding it ($2,800,000 × 90%).$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Defensive intangible asset
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Crane Corp. acquires Finley Inc. and obtains a patent that Crane does not intend to use but acquired to prevent competitors from using it. Under ASC 805, how should Crane account for this patent?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Recognize it as a separate identifiable intangible asset at fair value", "Exclude it from the balance sheet because Crane will not use it", "Include its value in goodwill because it lacks future economic benefit", "Expense it immediately as an acquisition cost"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): ASC 820-10-35-2C and ASC 805-20-25-10. A defensive intangible asset acquired in a business combination is recognized separately at fair value even though the acquirer does not intend to actively use it. Its fair value reflects the market participant's perspective. Wrong (B): Lack of active use by the acquirer does not prevent recognition; market participants would value the asset. Wrong (C): The patent meets the contractual-legal criterion and must be recognized separately from goodwill. Wrong (D): Acquired intangible assets are capitalized, not expensed; only transaction costs are expensed.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Goodwill impairment qualitative vs quantitative
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Sterling Corp. has a reporting unit with goodwill of $5,000,000. Sterling performs a qualitative assessment and considers macroeconomic conditions, industry trends, and the unit's financial performance. Sterling concludes it is not more likely than not that the reporting unit's fair value is less than its carrying amount. Which of the following best describes the appropriate next step?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Proceed to the quantitative impairment test to confirm the conclusion", "No further testing is required; document the qualitative conclusion", "Write down goodwill by a reasonable estimate", "Engage an independent appraiser to verify fair value"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): ASC 350-20-35-3A through 3G. If the qualitative assessment concludes it is not more likely than not (i.e., less than 50% likelihood) that fair value is below carrying amount, the entity is not required to proceed to the quantitative test. The qualitative assessment is sufficient. Wrong (A): The quantitative test is only required when qualitative factors indicate it is more likely than not that impairment exists. Wrong (C): A write-down cannot be recorded without completing the quantitative test to measure the impairment. Wrong (D): An independent appraisal is not required when the qualitative assessment indicates no impairment.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Measurement period adjustment effect on prior-period depreciation
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$On April 1, Year 1, Blake Corp. acquires Dover Inc. and provisionally records equipment at $2,000,000 with a 10-year useful life. In February Year 2 (within the measurement period), Blake determines the equipment's acquisition-date fair value was $2,400,000. What adjustment is required for Year 1 depreciation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No adjustment; prospective treatment only", "Increase Year 1 depreciation by $30,000 retroactively", "Increase Year 1 depreciation by $40,000 retroactively", "Decrease Year 1 depreciation by $30,000 retroactively"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): ASC 805-10-25-17. Measurement period adjustments are recorded retroactively as if known at the acquisition date. The additional $400,000 over 10 years = $40,000 additional annual depreciation. For the 9 months from acquisition (April–December Year 1): $40,000 × 9/12 = $30,000 retroactive increase. Wrong (A): Measurement period adjustments require retroactive treatment, not prospective. Wrong (C): The $40,000 figure is the full-year increase, but Year 1 only includes 9 months of depreciation from the April acquisition date. Wrong (D): Depreciation increases, not decreases, because the asset's fair value was higher than originally recorded.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Non-compete agreement classification and life
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Archer Corp. acquires Boone Inc. and recognizes a non-compete agreement with Boone's former owner valued at $750,000. The agreement has a contractual term of 5 years. Under ASC 805, how should Archer classify and subsequently account for this non-compete agreement?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Include it in goodwill and test annually for impairment", "Capitalize it as a cost of the acquisition", "Expense the $750,000 immediately as a period cost", "Recognize it as a finite-lived intangible asset and amortize over 5 years"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): ASC 805-20-55-14. A non-compete agreement arises from contractual rights and is recognized as a separate identifiable intangible asset. Because it has a finite contractual term (5 years), it is amortized over that useful life under ASC 350. Wrong (A): The non-compete meets the contractual-legal criterion and must be recognized separately from goodwill. Wrong (B): Identifiable intangible assets are recognized on the balance sheet, not treated as acquisition costs. Wrong (C): Acquired intangible assets that meet identification criteria are capitalized and amortized, not expensed immediately.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Acquisition without transferring consideration
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Jenner Corp. obtains a controlling financial interest in Pace Inc. solely through the lapse of minority veto rights held by other shareholders, without purchasing additional shares or transferring any consideration. Under ASC 805, how should Jenner account for this event?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Apply the acquisition method as of the date control is obtained", "No accounting entry is required since no consideration was transferred", "Record a memo entry only and disclose in the footnotes", "Recognize the investment using the equity method"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): ASC 805-10-25-10. A business combination can occur without transferring consideration, such as by contract alone or through lapse of veto rights. The acquisition method is still applied, measuring identifiable assets and liabilities at fair value on the date control is obtained. Wrong (B): The acquisition method applies regardless of whether consideration is transferred. Wrong (C): A memo entry is insufficient; full acquisition-method accounting is required. Wrong (D): Once control is obtained, the equity method is no longer appropriate; consolidation is required.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Pre-acquisition contingency recognition
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Coburn Corp. acquires Doyle Inc. At the acquisition date, Doyle has a pending product liability lawsuit. Coburn determines the fair value of the obligation is $800,000, though the liability does not meet the "probable" threshold under ASC 450. Under ASC 805, should Coburn recognize this contingency at the acquisition date?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No, because it does not meet the probable threshold under ASC 450", "Yes, at its fair value of $800,000 as part of the acquisition-date allocation", "Yes, but only as a disclosure in the footnotes", "No, unless the measurement period expires without resolution"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): ASC 805-20-25-19. Contingencies assumed in a business combination are recognized at acquisition-date fair value if fair value can be determined, regardless of whether the probable criterion under ASC 450 is met. This is a key difference from normal contingency accounting. Wrong (A): ASC 805 overrides the ASC 450 probability threshold at the acquisition date; fair value governs. Wrong (C): Disclosure alone is insufficient when fair value can be determined; the liability must be recognized. Wrong (D): The measurement period does not change the initial recognition requirement; fair value measurement applies at the acquisition date.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — Customer-related intangible asset
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$In its acquisition of Shelby Inc., Novak Corp. identifies a customer list that Shelby developed internally over 15 years. The list was never recorded on Shelby's books. Novak determines the customer list has a fair value of $1,200,000. How should Novak account for this customer list?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Include it in goodwill because Shelby never recorded it", "Expense it as an acquisition cost", "Recognize it as a separate intangible asset at $1,200,000", "Record it at Shelby's historical cost of zero"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): ASC 805-20-55-13. Customer lists are customer-related intangible assets that meet the separability criterion (they can be sold, licensed, or exchanged). They are recognized separately from goodwill at fair value, regardless of whether the acquiree previously recorded them. Wrong (A): An intangible asset's absence from the acquiree's books does not prevent recognition if it meets identifiability criteria. Wrong (B): Identified intangible assets are capitalized, not expensed as acquisition costs. Wrong (D): ASC 805 requires fair value measurement, not the acquiree's historical cost.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — Partial goodwill vs full goodwill method comparison
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Omega Corp. acquires 70% of Sigma Inc. for $14,000,000. The fair value of Sigma's identifiable net assets is $16,000,000. The fair value of the 30% NCI is $6,200,000. If Omega uses the proportionate share method instead of the fair value method for NCI, by how much will reported goodwill differ?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Goodwill is the same under both methods", "Goodwill is $1,400,000 lower under the proportionate share method", "Goodwill is $6,200,000 lower under the proportionate share method", "Goodwill is $2,000,000 lower under the proportionate share method"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the fair value method: Goodwill = $14,000,000 + $6,200,000 − $16,000,000 = $4,200,000. Under the proportionate share method: NCI = 30% × $16,000,000 = $4,800,000; Goodwill = $14,000,000 + $4,800,000 − $16,000,000 = $2,800,000. Difference = $4,200,000 − $2,800,000 = $1,400,000 lower. Wrong (A): The methods produce different goodwill amounts because NCI is measured differently. Wrong (C): This incorrectly uses the full NCI fair value as the difference. Wrong (D): $2,000,000 does not correspond to the correct calculation of the difference between the two methods.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Private company goodwill amortization election
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Under the private company alternative in ASC 350, what is the maximum amortization period for goodwill elected by a private company?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["10 years", "15 years", "20 years", "40 years"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): ASC 350-20-35-63. Under the private company alternative, entities may elect to amortize goodwill on a straight-line basis over 10 years or less if a shorter useful life is more appropriate. This is an irrevocable election. Wrong (B): The 15-year period applies to tax amortization of goodwill under Section 197, not the GAAP private company alternative. Wrong (C): Twenty years is not the maximum period under the private company goodwill alternative. Wrong (D): The 40-year maximum was from APB Opinion No. 17, which was superseded by ASC 350.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Marketing-related intangible asset classification
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$In a business combination, which category of identifiable intangible asset may have an indefinite useful life under ASC 805?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Customer-related intangibles", "Marketing-related intangibles", "Technology-based intangibles", "Contract-based intangibles"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): ASC 805-20-55-12 and ASC 350-30-35-4. Marketing-related intangible assets such as trademarks and trade names may have indefinite useful lives if there is no foreseeable limit on the period they will generate cash flows. They are not amortized but tested for impairment annually. Wrong (A): Customer-related intangibles (customer lists, order backlogs) typically have finite lives based on expected attrition. Wrong (C): Technology-based intangibles (patents, software) have finite lives due to technological obsolescence. Wrong (D): Contract-based intangibles have finite lives tied to the contractual term.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: easy / L2 — Inventory fair value in combination
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Nash Corp. acquires Elm Inc. in a business combination. Elm's finished goods inventory has a carrying value of $500,000 and a fair value of $620,000. How should Nash record this inventory at the acquisition date?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["At Elm's carrying value of $500,000", "At the lower of cost or net realizable value", "At fair value of $620,000", "At Elm's original manufacturing cost"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): ASC 805-20-30-1. All identifiable assets acquired in a business combination, including inventory, are measured at acquisition-date fair value. For finished goods, fair value typically approximates selling price less costs to sell and a reasonable profit margin. Wrong (A): The acquiree's carrying value is not used; fair value governs under ASC 805. Wrong (B): The lower of cost or NRV rule applies to the acquirer's subsequent accounting, not the initial acquisition-date measurement. Wrong (D): Original manufacturing cost is irrelevant to fair value measurement at the acquisition date.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q24: easy / L2 — Intercompany balances elimination in consolidation
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$After acquiring 100% of Troy Inc., Pike Corp. has a $2,000,000 intercompany receivable from Troy. When preparing the consolidated balance sheet, how should Pike treat this intercompany balance?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Report it as a current asset on the consolidated balance sheet", "Reclassify it as a noncurrent intercompany asset", "Write it off as a bad debt loss in the consolidated statements", "Eliminate it entirely as part of the consolidation process"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): ASC 810-10-45-1. In preparing consolidated financial statements, all intercompany balances (receivables and payables) between the parent and its subsidiaries are eliminated. These transactions do not exist from the perspective of the consolidated entity. Wrong (A): Intercompany balances are not reported as external assets in consolidated statements. Wrong (B): Reclassification does not resolve the intercompany nature of the balance. Wrong (C): Elimination is not a loss; it is a consolidation procedure that removes internal transactions.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Order backlog intangible asset
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Under ASC 805, an order or production backlog acquired in a business combination is classified as which type of intangible asset?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A contract-based intangible asset", "A technology-based intangible asset", "A marketing-related intangible asset", "An unidentifiable intangible included in goodwill"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): ASC 805-20-55-14. An order or production backlog arises from contracts such as purchase or sales orders and is classified as a contract-based intangible asset. It meets the contractual-legal criterion for separate recognition. Wrong (B): Order backlogs are not technology-based; they arise from customer contracts, not technical innovations. Wrong (C): Marketing-related intangibles include trademarks and trade names, not order backlogs. Wrong (D): Order backlogs meet the contractual-legal criterion and are recognized separately from goodwill.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: medium / L3 — Goodwill allocation to reporting units
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Greer Corp. completes a business combination and recognizes $6,000,000 of goodwill. The acquired business will be integrated into two of Greer's existing reporting units: Unit A (which will receive 60% of the synergies) and Unit B (40%). How should Greer allocate the goodwill?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Allocate equally: $3,000,000 to each unit", "Allocate $3,600,000 to Unit A and $2,400,000 to Unit B based on expected synergy benefits", "Allocate all $6,000,000 to the larger reporting unit", "Do not allocate until the first impairment test"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): ASC 350-20-35-41. Goodwill is assigned to reporting units that are expected to benefit from the synergies of the business combination. The allocation is based on the relative benefit expected, resulting in $6,000,000 × 60% = $3,600,000 to Unit A and $6,000,000 × 40% = $2,400,000 to Unit B. Wrong (A): Equal allocation is not appropriate when the expected benefits differ between reporting units. Wrong (C): Allocating entirely to the larger unit ignores the benefit received by the other unit. Wrong (D): Goodwill must be assigned to reporting units at the acquisition date, not deferred until impairment testing.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — Acquiree employee benefit obligation assumed
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$During its acquisition of Page Inc., Culver Corp. assumes Page's defined benefit pension obligation. The projected benefit obligation has a fair value of $8,000,000 and the plan assets have a fair value of $6,500,000. How should Culver recognize the pension in the acquisition-date allocation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Recognize only the plan assets of $6,500,000", "Record the obligation and assets at Page's book values", "Recognize a net pension liability of $1,500,000 at fair value", "Offset the pension items against goodwill"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): ASC 805-20-30-1 and ASC 715-30. The acquirer recognizes the net pension liability (PBO of $8,000,000 less plan assets of $6,500,000 = $1,500,000) at fair value. Any previously existing amounts in accumulated other comprehensive income on the acquiree's books are not carried forward. Wrong (A): Both the obligation and plan assets must be recognized; reporting only assets understates the liability. Wrong (B): Fair value, not book value, governs all measurements in a business combination. Wrong (D): The net pension liability is a separately identifiable liability and is not subsumed within goodwill.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: hard / L4 — Goodwill tax deductibility book-tax difference
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Fletcher Corp. acquires 100% of Holt Inc. in a stock acquisition (tax-free reorganization) for $15,000,000. The fair value of Holt's identifiable net assets is $12,000,000. For book purposes, Fletcher recognizes $3,000,000 of goodwill. For tax purposes, no goodwill is deductible because the acquisition is tax-free. Assuming a 25% tax rate, what deferred tax consequence arises from the goodwill?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A deferred tax asset of $750,000", "A deferred tax liability of $750,000", "Both a deferred tax asset and liability of $375,000 each", "No deferred tax is recognized for the goodwill difference"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): ASC 805-740-25-8 and ASC 740-10-25-3(e). A deferred tax asset is not recognized for the excess of book goodwill over tax-deductible goodwill (here tax goodwill is zero). This is a specific exception under ASC 740 to prevent "grossing up" goodwill with a related deferred tax asset. Wrong (A): The exception in ASC 740 prohibits recognizing a DTA for non-deductible goodwill. Wrong (B): A DTL would apply if tax goodwill exceeded book goodwill, which is not the case here. Wrong (C): No deferred tax is recognized in either direction for this initial goodwill difference.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q29: hard / L4 — Acquiree operating lease right-of-use asset remeasurement
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Whitfield Corp. acquires Trent Inc. Trent has an operating lease as lessee with 6 years remaining. The lease has above-market terms — Trent pays $120,000 annually while the current market rate for a comparable lease is $90,000 annually. Under ASC 805 and ASC 842, how does this affect the acquisition-date accounting for the lease?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Recognize an unfavorable lease liability at the present value of the $30,000 annual excess", "Remeasure the lease liability and right-of-use asset at fair value as if it were a new lease", "Carry forward the acquiree's existing lease balances with no adjustment", "Record the right-of-use asset at fair value, which reflects the unfavorable terms"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): ASC 805-20-30-1 and ASC 842-10-55-12. In a business combination, the acquiree's right-of-use asset is measured at the same amount as the lease liability, adjusted to reflect favorable or unfavorable terms relative to market. An above-market lease reduces the ROU asset below the liability amount to reflect the unfavorable terms at fair value. Wrong (A): ASC 805 no longer requires separate intangible assets or liabilities for favorable/unfavorable leases; the adjustment is embedded in the ROU asset. Wrong (B): The lease liability is remeasured using the remaining payments but the ROU asset is not simply set equal to it. Wrong (C): ASC 805 requires fair value measurement of all identifiable assets and liabilities, including lease elements.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Technology-based intangible finite life amortization
(4, 'Business Combinations', $EXPL$Porter Corp. acquires Vance Inc. and separately recognizes developed technology valued at $3,600,000 with an estimated useful life of 8 years and a trade name valued at $1,000,000 with an indefinite useful life. What is the total annual amortization expense from these acquired intangible assets?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$575,000", "$450,000", "$383,333", "$266,667"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): ASC 350-30-35-1 and 35-4. Developed technology is a finite-lived intangible amortized over its useful life: $3,600,000 / 8 = $450,000 per year. The trade name has an indefinite life and is not amortized but tested for impairment annually. Total annual amortization = $450,000. Wrong (A): This incorrectly amortizes both assets ($3,600,000 + $1,000,000) / 8 = $575,000, but indefinite-lived intangibles are not amortized. Wrong (C): This amortizes the combined total over 12 years, which is incorrect. Wrong (D): This incorrectly amortizes only the trade name over some period and excludes the technology.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
